&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The main lesson
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The main lesson from this project is simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;E2EE is an architecture decision, not a library.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You cannot take a normal Spring Boot chat and just "turn encryption on".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the backend is not supposed to be trusted, that decision affects everything:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;database schema;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;API shape;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;WebSocket topics;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;multi-device delivery;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;message previews;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;logs;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;file attachments;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;device management;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;frontend state;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;threat model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A messenger is not just "a chat with WebSocket".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an E2EE model, it is a system for delivering encrypted envelopes to specific devices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once that idea becomes clear, many strange-looking implementation decisions start to make sense.&lt;/p&gt;




